            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither is better for everyone. Whey is complete, rich in leucine, usually cheaper and mixes most easily, so it suits most beginners who tolerate dairy. Plant blends suit vegans, people with milk allergies and anyone who prefers to avoid dairy, though they tend to be thicker and earthier. A blend of several plant sources gets closest to whey's amino acid profile."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many scoops of protein powder should I take a day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Start with one scoop a day. Most adults who train benefit from roughly 1.2 to 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight daily, and food usually covers most of that. Estimate what you already eat, then add one 20 to 30 g serving to close the gap. Check with a doctor first if you have kidney disease or are pregnant."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I take protein powder if I don't work out?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Protein powder is just a convenient food, and people who do not exercise still need protein. It can help older adults, busy people and those who eat little at breakfast reach a sensible daily intake. Without resistance training it will not build much muscle, though, so do not expect a physique change from the powder alone."
          }
        },
